    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "hint",
        "explainCode",
        "buildHTML",
        "buildCSS"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
        "buildCSS": "- Do NOT use separate file name. Put all css in a `<style>` tag in the head of the index.html file\n- Do NOT use relative units. Use px values.\n- You should generate both HTML and CSS at the same time\n- Keep all explanations short, concise, and simple.",
        "buildHTML": "- Do not create a style.css file - put all css updates in a <style> tag in the head.\n- Do NOT use IDs or class on elements\n- Do NOT generate links or buttons\n- Generate both HTML and CSS at the same time\n- Never generate placeholder images or videos. If the user implies they want an image, generate an empty `<img src=\"\" />` tag and tell the user they need to upload images to their project.\n- If the user asks to generate images: explain that you cannot generate new images but can insert any images that the user uploads to the project\n- If the user asks to insert an image from an external URL: explain that for security reasons, you cannot use external URLs for images. Instead, suggest that the user will eventually be able to upload their own images that can be added to webpages.\n- Keep all explanations short, concise, and simple."
      }
    },
    "project_template_level_name": "hoai-web-design-v2-template1_pilot2_launch",
    "name_suffix": "_launch",
    "offer_browser_tts": "true",
    "teacher_markdown": "**Intent:** This is the culmination of the progression - students apply everything they've learned in service of a page they built and care about. There is no single prescribed path and no defined completion point. The level instructions explicitly tell students \"you decide when you're done,\" which is intentional. Watch for students who are paralyzed by this openness - they may need help articulating one specific thing they want to improve before they can move forward.\r\n\r\n**Circulation questions:**\r\n\r\n- \"What's one thing about your page that still doesn't match what you had in your head?\"  \r\n- \"What approach are you taking - building something new, fixing something broken, or refining something that's close?\"  \r\n- \"What did you have to learn - or say differently - to make that change happen?\"\r\n\r\n**Expected mistakes and misconceptions:**\r\n\r\n- Students may make changes without a clear goal - ask them to state one specific intention before prompting: \"what exactly are you trying to change, and why?\"  \r\n- Students may rely entirely on AI without evaluating outputs - prompt the Accept/Reject habit: \"before you accept that, does it match what you asked for?\"  \r\n- Students may feel like their page isn't \"good enough\" - remind them the goal is intentional iteration, not perfection: \"did you close the gap between your vision and your page? That's what matters.\"  \r\n- Students who finish quickly may not have iterated meaningfully - ask them to compare their current version to their Level 3 version and explain what changed and why  \r\n- Students may not know they can revert using Version History if something goes wrong - proactively mention this as you circulate, especially for students who seem hesitant to make bold changes",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "false"
  },
